// Chapter 7: Friday breakfast, in English. Translated by Claude Code from chapter7.ink; it follows that file
// line for line (same knots, lines, speakers and choice ids; the parity test in Story/Tests checks it). Change the
// dialect file first, then this one. [[word]] marks a tap-to-explain word; its English meaning is in the Glossary table.
// Teta's song in memory 7 is invented for the game: no real song is named or quoted.

EXTERNAL chose(knot)

=== long_table ===
One long table, from the door to the counter. And the whole street wants the same chair. #sys
I'm sitting here, by the window. This has been my spot since I was little. #samira
Samira, this has been my spot since '74. You used to sit on the floor. #abu_fadi #smile
And I'll sit wherever the chair doesn't creak. #karim
Fine, shall we draw lots? #lana #thinking
No lots, nothing. Teta used to say: a long table has no head. #salma #smile
-> END

=== breakfast_counter ===
Good morning, girls! I brought you things from my kitchen: [[galayet bandora]], [[makdous]], and fava beans with lemon. #um_rami #smile
Taste before you talk. And if you don't like it, talk after. #um_rami
What's this? Eggplant stuffed with walnuts?… Good. Really good. #lana #surprised
Rami won't eat his beans without extra lemon. Everything I make, I make the way he likes it. #um_rami
Um Rami, if that's how it is, Friday breakfast isn't complete without your tray. #salma #smile
-> END

=== street_tables ===
The tables are out on the pavement, and the postman has sat down at one. It's the first time he's ever sat. #sys
Usually I drop off the letters and go. But today… the tea is good. #postman #smile
Stay as long as you like, Uncle. The pavement is yours as much as ours. #salma
Your Teta used to say: the postman carries everyone's news, so who carries his? #postman
There's a story I have to tell you, my girl… but not today. #postman #sad
-> END

=== radio_shelf ===
I found it under the counter, wrapped in a sheet. Does it still work, Uncle? #salma
Try it. She listened to it every Friday, from first light until Friday prayers. #abu_fadi
Salma turns the dial. Static… then a faraway voice, as if it's coming from long ago. #sys
She had one song she waited for every Friday. If it didn't come on, she'd say the radio was sulking. #abu_fadi #smile
What song? #lana
You'll know it when you hear it, my girl. #abu_fadi
-> END

=== bread_basket ===
You know what? Every night I used to open the airline app and look at flights to Riyadh. #lana
And now? #salma
I haven't opened it in two weeks. I only noticed today. #lana #thinking
So Amman's bread did its job. #salma #smile
Don't make a big thing of it. Just pass me a [[ka'ak]], one of the sesame ones. #lana #smile
-> END

=== friday_breakfast ===
The first Friday breakfast. The long table is full, and the radio is playing on its shelf. #sys
This is how Fridays used to be. Everyone, I swear on my children, everyone. #samira #smile
Just two missing. #abu_fadi #sad
You mean my mother and my aunt? #lana #sad
Breakfast is long, my girl. There's time. #abu_fadi
-> END

=== m7_lead ===
She'd stand right here in front of the radio and turn the dial slowly… as if she were pouring coffee. #abu_fadi
And when she found her song, the whole café went quiet. Even me. #abu_fadi #smile
-> END

=== m7_intro ===
Fadi, the radio's lost the station again! And the song starts any minute. #teta_young #surprised
Give it here, Im Khalil. I'll find it. #fadi_young
You? All you ever find is the weather and the football. #teta_young #smile
Gently… a radio is like a [[dallah]]: rush it and it sulks. #teta_young
-> END

=== m7_perfect ===
It's clear! Listen… “Cardamom of the morning, tell the one who's far away: your cup is waiting…” #teta_young #smile
-> END

=== m7_ok ===
It's not clear… never mind, I know the words by heart. I'll sing the rest myself. #teta_young #smile
-> END

=== m7_outro ===
The whole street goes quiet when you sing, Im Khalil. #fadi_young
This is my song. Every Friday I'll sing it, until my children come and sing it with me. #teta_young #smile
And every Friday, for years, the radio stayed on the same shelf. And in its back, Teta hid a folded paper. #sys
-> END

=== m7_return ===
Salma, there's something taped to the back of the radio. A folded paper, with my name and yours on it. #lana #surprised
Teta's handwriting! #salma #surprised
She always hid her dearest things behind the things she loved. #abu_fadi #smile
-> END

=== letter_4 ===
To Salma and Lana, the daughters of my daughters: #letter
Abu Fadi: his cup with cardamom, his table by the window. And on Fridays, leave him to his silence. #letter
Samira: ask how she is, not how her children are. #letter
Karim: don't give him money; he won't take it. Feed him lunch. #letter
Um Rami: buy from her, and never call it charity. #letter
The postman: if he ever sits down, hear him out to the end. #letter
Mrs. Odeh: she'll ask you about Rana. Answer her. #letter
These are my people. The rest you'll come to know the way I did: one cup at a time. #letter
And if one day someone asks you about the knafeh… #letter_faded
— Your Teta #letter_signature
-> END

=== voice_4 ===
My girls, my hearts… Friday breakfast is for everyone. And whoever isn't coming, set a plate for them anyway. They'll come. #teta
-> END

=== notify_4 ===
Samira sent me a photo of the breakfast. Your Teta put the oil and [[za'atar]] in the middle of the table, not at the end. #rana
-> END
